Distributed Web Infrastructure
===============================

Overview
--------
This is a three-server web infrastructure hosting www.foobar.com:
1 load balancer (HAProxy) and 2 servers, each containing a web server
(Nginx), an application server, the application code, and a MySQL
database (configured as a Primary-Replica cluster).

Components added and why
-------------------------
- Load balancer (HAProxy): distributes incoming traffic across the two
  servers so no single server is overwhelmed, and removes the
  single-server SPOF for traffic handling.
- 2nd server (web server + app server + codebase + database): adds
  redundancy - if one server goes down, the other can continue to
  serve traffic, improving availability and allowing horizontal
  scaling.
- Primary-Replica database setup: one database (Primary) handles
  writes, and the other (Replica) handles reads, improving
  performance and providing a backup copy of the data.

Load balancer algorithm
------------------------
The load balancer is configured with the Round Robin algorithm: each
new incoming request is sent to the next server in the list, cycling
back to the first server after reaching the last one. This spreads
traffic evenly across both servers.

Active-Active vs Active-Passive
--------------------------------
This setup is Active-Active: both servers behind the load balancer are
actively running and receiving traffic at the same time.
In an Active-Passive setup, only one server (active) handles traffic
while the other (passive) stays idle as a standby, only taking over if
the active server fails.
The difference: Active-Active uses all servers simultaneously (better
resource usage), while Active-Passive keeps a server in reserve purely
for failover (simpler, but wastes idle capacity).

Primary-Replica (Master-Slave) database cluster
--------------------------------------------------
In a Primary-Replica cluster, the Primary node receives all write
operations (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE). Those changes are then
replicated (copied) to one or more Replica nodes. Replica nodes keep
an up-to-date copy of the data and are typically used to serve read
operations, which reduces the load on the Primary.

Difference between Primary and Replica (from the application's view)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
The application sends all write queries to the Primary node, and can
send read queries to either the Primary or a Replica node. The
application must be configured to know which node is the Primary (for
writes) and which are Replicas (for reads) - writing to a Replica is
not allowed/supported.

Issues with this infrastructure
--------------------------------
Where are the SPOFs?
- The load balancer itself is a SPOF: if it goes down, no traffic can
  reach either server.
- The Primary database node is a SPOF for writes: if it goes down,
  no new write operations can be processed until a Replica is
  promoted.

Security issues:
- No firewall: the servers are exposed to any traffic from the
  internet, including malicious traffic, with nothing filtering or
  blocking unwanted connections.
- No HTTPS: traffic between the user and the servers is unencrypted
  (plain HTTP), meaning data (including sensitive data) can be
  intercepted or tampered with in transit.

No monitoring:
There is no monitoring in place, so there's no visibility into server
health, performance, traffic, or errors - issues could go unnoticed
until the website is already down or degraded for users.
